# Reopen-blank fix — PREVIOUS architecture (client-owned snapshot)

> What we built first, then moved away from. Captured here so the contrast is clear.

## Idea in one line
The **client** (the renderer / xterm window) takes a picture of its own screen, stores it in **browser localStorage**, and paints it back on reopen.

## Where the "last screen" lives — on the CLIENT

```mermaid
flowchart TB
  subgraph CLIENT["CLIENT (renderer)"]
    direction TB
    XT["xterm screen"]
    LS["localStorage<br/>(per browser, per device)"]
    RC["reconcile + provider table<br/>+ fallback spinner"]
    XT -->|"serialize()"| LS
    LS -->|"read &rarr; repaint"| XT
    RC -.->|"clear/replace logic"| XT
  end
  subgraph SERVER["MAIN / future terminal server"]
    PTY["PTY + output buffer<br/>(real source of truth)"]
  end
  CLIENT <-->|"thin pipe"| SERVER
  classDef bad fill:#5b2330,stroke:#e0506a,color:#ffd7df;
  class LS,RC bad
```

State + logic pile up **inside the client box**. The server owns the session but not its screen.

## Flow

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant U as User
  participant C as Client (xterm)
  participant S as Server (PTY)
  rect rgb(40,40,48)
  note over C,S: Hibernate
  C->>C: serialize screen &rarr; localStorage
  S->>S: kill PTY (in-memory buffer lost)
  end
  rect rgb(40,40,48)
  note over C,S: Reopen
  U->>C: click Reopen
  C->>C: read localStorage &rarr; repaint (instant)
  S->>S: spawn CLI --resume
  S-->>C: reprint transcript (live)
  C->>C: reconcile: clear old screen / replace
  end
```

## Why we moved away from it
The terminal process is being **moved to a separate server**. That makes this design fight the direction:

- 🔴 **State on the wrong side.** Session history lives in the *client's* localStorage. A reconnect, a second window, or a web client sees **nothing**.
- 🔴 **Lossy copy.** The client re-derives a screen via xterm; the server already holds the **real output bytes**.
- 🔴 **Logic drift.** "Which providers reprint on resume" lives in the **server's** adapters, but the client kept its own copy.
- 🔴 **Heavy client.** Capture + store + restore + reconcile + a fallback spinner — all on the renderer.

**Verdict:** works in one desktop window, but accumulates responsibility on the client right as the architecture is trying to thin it. → replaced by the server-owned design (other artifact).
